  url = {https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/25A312},
  urldate = {2026-08-29},
  note = {Interim docket; the disposition is “Application for stay denied.” 5--4. Upholds the Federal Reserve Act's for-cause removal protection, on the Fed's descent from the First and Second Banks of the United States, its budget free of congressional control and its privately owned regional banks, and holds that a Governor is owed notice and a hearing before removal. The case is disposed of narrowly, on the ground that Cook did not receive the process the statute required: the Court states that it has “not addressed the facts, as they have yet to be found or analyzed under the relevant legal standards,” and Kavanaugh, J., concurring, opens that “[t]oday's interim ruling does not decide whether the President may lawfully remove Governor Cook for cause.” The dispute is live — on 5 August 2026 the President notified Cook that he was again considering her removal, giving her 21 days to respond and citing this opinion as his authority for the procedure.}
}

