  note      = {Cited for the design principles of long-enduring common-pool resource institutions, Table~3.1, p.~90, in chapter~3 (pp.~58--102). Her numbering: (1) clearly defined boundaries, “Individuals or households who have rights to withdraw resource units from the CPR must be clearly defined, as must the boundaries of the CPR itself”; (2) congruence between appropriation and provision rules and local conditions; (3) collective-choice arrangements, “most individuals affected by the operational rules can participate in modifying the operational rules”; (4) monitoring, by monitors who “are accountable to the appropriators or are the appropriators”; (5) graduated sanctions; (6) conflict-resolution mechanisms, low-cost local arenas; (7) minimal recognition of rights to organize, by external government; and, “For CPRs that are parts of larger systems,” (8) nested enterprises, “governance activities are organized in multiple layers of nested enterprises.” The quotations of principles~3 and~8 are as Cox, Arnold and Villamayor Tomás, Ecology and Society 15(4):~38 (2010), give them with the page reference 1990:90; that of principle~1 and the wording of the others are from a transcription of Table~3.1 on publiccommons.ca, which has small copying errors elsewhere.}
